## §1 — High-level picture: what Redis is and how it works

Before any commands, understand what Redis **does** and **why** it exists.

### What Redis is

Redis is an **in-memory data structure store**. It keeps all data in **RAM**, which makes it extremely fast (sub-millisecond reads/writes). It can act as:

1. **Cache** — store frequently accessed data so your app doesn't hit a slow database every time.
2. **Message broker** — pub/sub channels or list-based queues (Celery uses Redis this way).
3. **Primary database** — with persistence enabled, Redis can be a real data store (not just throwaway cache).
4. **Session store** — web sessions, tokens, rate-limit counters.

### How it works (simplified)

1. Your **application** connects to Redis over **TCP port 6379**.
2. App sends a **command** in the RESP (Redis Serialization Protocol) text format — e.g. `SET user:123 "Alice"`.
3. Redis processes it **single-threaded** on the main event loop (no locks, no race conditions for individual commands).
4. Redis replies immediately from RAM — e.g. `OK` or the value `"Alice"`.
5. Optionally, Redis **persists** to disk (RDB snapshots and/or AOF append log) so data survives restarts.

### Why single-threaded matters

Redis processes commands **one at a time** on its main thread. This means:
- No locking, no deadlocks, no race conditions on a single instance.
- **Slow commands** (e.g. `KEYS *` on millions of keys) **block everything** — there's no other thread to handle other clients while that runs.
- Throughput is bounded by **single-core speed** — but in practice Redis handles 100k+ ops/sec because RAM access is nanoseconds.
- Redis 6+ uses **I/O threads** for network read/write (parsing, sending responses) but command execution remains single-threaded.

### Diagram

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph clients [Application layer]
    App1[App server 1]
    App2[App server 2]
    Celery[Celery worker]
  end
  subgraph redis_node [Redis server]
    EVLoop["Single-threaded event loop"]
    RAM[In-memory data]
    Disk["Disk (RDB + AOF)"]
    EVLoop --> RAM
    RAM -->|"periodic snapshot / append"| Disk
  end
  App1 -->|"TCP 6379"| EVLoop
  App2 -->|"TCP 6379"| EVLoop
  Celery -->|"TCP 6379"| EVLoop
```

**Key insight:** Redis is **fast because of RAM, simple because of single-threaded execution, and durable because of optional disk persistence.** Most problems come from misunderstanding one of these three.

