## §4 — Architecture patterns (when to use what)

### Standalone

One Redis instance. Simplest. Good for: dev, small caches, Celery broker where loss on restart is acceptable.

### Master + Replica(s)

One master (writes) + N replicas (reads). Replicas are async copies. Good for: read-heavy workloads, simple HA with manual failover.

### Master + Replica + Sentinel

Same as above but **Sentinel** automates failover. Good for: production HA where you can't afford manual intervention. Minimum 3 Sentinel processes.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  subgraph sentinel_quorum [Sentinel quorum]
    S1[Sentinel 1]
    S2[Sentinel 2]
    S3[Sentinel 3]
  end
  M[Master 6379]
  R1[Replica 1]
  R2[Replica 2]
  S1 -->|monitor| M
  S2 -->|monitor| M
  S3 -->|monitor| M
  M -->|replication| R1
  M -->|replication| R2
  S1 -.->|failover: promote| R1
```

### Redis Cluster

Multiple masters, each owning a subset of 16384 hash slots. Each master has replicas. Good for: data bigger than one machine's RAM, or write throughput beyond one node.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph cluster [Redis Cluster]
    M1["Master 1 (slots 0-5460)"]
    M2["Master 2 (slots 5461-10922)"]
    M3["Master 3 (slots 10923-16383)"]
    R1[Replica of M1]
    R2[Replica of M2]
    R3[Replica of M3]
    M1 --> R1
    M2 --> R2
    M3 --> R3
  end
  Client[App] -->|MOVED redirect| M1
  Client -->|MOVED redirect| M2
  Client -->|MOVED redirect| M3
```

### Decision table

| Scenario | Pattern | Why |
|----------|---------|-----|
| Dev / local / throwaway cache | Standalone | Simplest |
| Celery broker (small team) | Standalone or K8s single pod | Loss on restart OK; Celery retries |
| Production cache (read-heavy) | Master + Replica + Sentinel | HA + read scale |
| Session store (must not lose data) | Master + Replica + Sentinel + AOF | Durability + HA |
| Data > single node RAM | Redis Cluster | Horizontal sharding |
| Multi-region | Redis Cluster or vendor (ElastiCache, Azure Cache) | Built-in geo support |

