## §7 — Common issues and why they happen

### OOM (Out of Memory) kill

**Why:** `maxmemory` not set (or set too high). Redis keeps growing. Linux OOM killer terminates it. All cached data gone. Apps get connection refused.  
**Fix:** Always set `maxmemory` below available RAM (leave room for OS, RDB fork, etc.). Choose an eviction policy (`allkeys-lru` for cache, `noeviction` if you need data integrity).

### RDB fork failure (can't save)

**Why:** RDB snapshot uses `fork()`. The forked process needs **copy-on-write** pages. If the system has insufficient RAM or `vm.overcommit_memory` is `0`, fork fails → `MISCONF Redis is configured to save RDB snapshots, but it's currently unable to persist to disk`.  
**Fix:** Set `vm.overcommit_memory=1` in sysctl. Ensure available RAM > `used_memory` (for worst-case COW). Or disable RDB if using AOF only.

### Latency spike from slow commands

**Why:** Someone runs `KEYS *` or `SMEMBERS` on a set with millions of members. Redis is single-threaded — this blocks **all** other clients for seconds.  
**Fix:** `rename-command KEYS ""` in production. Use `SCAN` instead of `KEYS`. Monitor `SLOWLOG`. Avoid O(N) commands on large collections.

### Replication lag / split-brain

**Why:** Replica falls behind master because of network issues or replica doing a full resync (RDB transfer). If Sentinel promotes a lagging replica, recent writes are lost.  
**Also:** Sentinel quorum of 2 in a 2-node setup → split-brain. Always use **odd** number of Sentinels (minimum 3).  
**Fix:** Monitor `master_repl_offset` vs `slave_repl_offset`. Set `min-replicas-to-write 1` + `min-replicas-max-lag 10` on master to reject writes if no healthy replica exists.

### Connection exhaustion

**Why:** App creates a new TCP connection per request instead of using a **connection pool**. Redis `maxclients` (default 10000) fills up. New connections rejected.  
**Fix:** Use connection pooling in your client library. Monitor `connected_clients` in INFO. Set `timeout` in redis.conf to close idle connections.

### Thundering herd (cache stampede)

**Why:** A popular cache key expires. Hundreds of app servers simultaneously miss the cache and all hit the database to regenerate it. Database overloaded.  
**Fix:** Use a **lock** (Redis-based distributed lock) so only one client regenerates the cache. Or use probabilistic early expiration (refresh key *before* TTL hits).

### Data loss on restart (no persistence)

**Why:** Redis started with default config, `appendonly no`, no `save` directives. Restart → empty database. App serves stale or empty responses.  
**Fix:** Enable AOF (`appendonly yes`) for durability. For pure cache, ensure app handles cache miss gracefully.

### Cluster: CROSSSLOT error

**Why:** Multi-key command (`MGET key1 key2`) where keys hash to **different slots** across different masters. Redis Cluster rejects it.  
**Fix:** Use **hash tags** — `{user}:profile` and `{user}:settings` both hash on `user`, landing on the same slot.

