## §8 — CLI cheatsheet

### Connection and server

```bash
redis-cli ping                          # PONG
redis-cli -h <host> -p 6379 -a <pass>  # remote with auth
redis-cli INFO                          # all server info
redis-cli INFO memory                   # memory section only
redis-cli INFO replication              # replication status
redis-cli INFO keyspace                 # DB key counts
redis-cli CONFIG GET maxmemory          # read config
redis-cli CONFIG SET maxmemory 4gb      # live config change (no restart)
redis-cli CONFIG REWRITE                # persist live changes to redis.conf
redis-cli DBSIZE                        # key count in current DB
redis-cli SELECT 1                      # switch to DB 1
```

### Key operations

```bash
redis-cli SET mykey "hello" EX 3600     # set with 1h TTL
redis-cli GET mykey
redis-cli DEL mykey
redis-cli EXISTS mykey
redis-cli TTL mykey                     # seconds remaining (-1 = no expire, -2 = gone)
redis-cli EXPIRE mykey 600              # set TTL on existing key
redis-cli TYPE mykey                    # string, list, set, zset, hash, stream
redis-cli SCAN 0 MATCH "session:*" COUNT 100   # safe iteration (not KEYS)
```

### Strings and counters

```bash
redis-cli INCR page:views               # atomic increment
redis-cli INCRBY page:views 10
redis-cli MSET k1 v1 k2 v2             # multi-set
redis-cli MGET k1 k2                   # multi-get
redis-cli SETEX token:abc 1800 "data"  # set + expire in one command
```

### Hashes

```bash
redis-cli HSET user:42 name "Alice" email "a@b.com"
redis-cli HGET user:42 name
redis-cli HGETALL user:42
redis-cli HDEL user:42 email
redis-cli HINCRBY user:42 login_count 1
```

### Lists (queues)

```bash
redis-cli LPUSH queue:tasks "task1"     # push left
redis-cli RPOP queue:tasks              # pop right (FIFO)
redis-cli BRPOP queue:tasks 30          # blocking pop (30s timeout) — Celery uses this
redis-cli LRANGE queue:tasks 0 -1       # list all items
redis-cli LLEN queue:tasks              # queue depth
```

### Sets

```bash
redis-cli SADD tags:post:1 "redis" "cache" "database"
redis-cli SMEMBERS tags:post:1
redis-cli SCARD tags:post:1             # count
redis-cli SINTER tags:post:1 tags:post:2   # intersection
```

### Sorted sets

```bash
redis-cli ZADD leaderboard 100 "alice" 200 "bob"
redis-cli ZRANGE leaderboard 0 -1 WITHSCORES
redis-cli ZRANGEBYSCORE leaderboard 50 150
redis-cli ZINCRBY leaderboard 10 "alice"
redis-cli ZRANK leaderboard "alice"     # rank (0-based)
```

### Streams

```bash
redis-cli XADD events * type "login" user "alice"
redis-cli XLEN events
redis-cli XRANGE events - + COUNT 10
redis-cli XGROUP CREATE events mygroup $ MKSTREAM
redis-cli XREADGROUP GROUP mygroup consumer1 COUNT 1 BLOCK 5000 STREAMS events >
redis-cli XACK events mygroup <id>
redis-cli XINFO STREAM events
redis-cli XINFO GROUPS events
```

### Pub/Sub

```bash
# Terminal 1: subscribe
redis-cli SUBSCRIBE mychannel

# Terminal 2: publish
redis-cli PUBLISH mychannel "hello subscribers"
```

### Monitoring and debug

```bash
redis-cli MONITOR                       # live command stream (CAREFUL in prod — adds overhead)
redis-cli SLOWLOG GET 10                # last 10 slow commands
redis-cli SLOWLOG RESET
redis-cli CLIENT LIST                   # all connected clients
redis-cli CLIENT KILL ID <id>           # disconnect a client
redis-cli MEMORY USAGE mykey            # bytes used by one key
redis-cli MEMORY DOCTOR                 # memory health advice
redis-cli DEBUG SLEEP 0                 # DON'T run in prod
redis-cli LATENCY LATEST                # latency events (if enabled)
redis-cli OBJECT ENCODING mykey         # internal encoding (ziplist, hashtable, etc.)
```

### Persistence operations

```bash
redis-cli BGSAVE                        # trigger RDB snapshot
redis-cli BGREWRITEAOF                  # compact AOF
redis-cli LASTSAVE                      # timestamp of last successful RDB
redis-cli CONFIG GET save               # current RDB triggers
redis-cli CONFIG SET save ""            # disable RDB (live)
```

### Replication

```bash
redis-cli INFO replication              # role, connected_slaves, offsets
redis-cli REPLICAOF <master-ip> 6379    # make this node a replica (live)
redis-cli REPLICAOF NO ONE              # promote replica to master (live)
```

### Sentinel

```bash
redis-cli -p 26379 SENTINEL masters     # list monitored masters
redis-cli -p 26379 SENTINEL master mymaster   # detail on one master
redis-cli -p 26379 SENTINEL replicas mymaster
redis-cli -p 26379 SENTINEL failover mymaster   # force failover
redis-cli -p 26379 SENTINEL get-master-addr-by-name mymaster   # current master IP:port
```

### Cluster

```bash
redis-cli --cluster create <ip1>:6379 <ip2>:6379 <ip3>:6379 \
  <ip4>:6379 <ip5>:6379 <ip6>:6379 --cluster-replicas 1
redis-cli CLUSTER INFO
redis-cli CLUSTER NODES
redis-cli CLUSTER SLOTS
redis-cli --cluster check <ip>:6379
redis-cli --cluster reshard <ip>:6379
redis-cli --cluster rebalance <ip>:6379
```

### Dangerous commands (know them, avoid in prod)

```bash
redis-cli KEYS *                        # O(N) scan — blocks everything. Use SCAN instead.
redis-cli FLUSHDB                       # delete all keys in current DB
redis-cli FLUSHALL                      # delete all keys in ALL DBs
redis-cli SHUTDOWN NOSAVE               # stop without saving
```

